description:
In liquidatePosition() at line 318, the code computes borrowedValue as:
  uint256 borrowedValue = owedAmount * bPrice / ERC20(up.denomination).decimals();
ERC20.decimals() returns a uint8 (e.g., 6 for USDC, 18 for WETH, 8 for WBTC). The correct formula should divide by 10**decimals(), not by decimals() itself. Dividing by 6 instead of 1e6 makes borrowedValue ~166,666x too large. This inflates borrowedValue so that totalValueUSD > borrowedValue is almost never true (or far less often), preventing the protocol fee from being collected. More critically, this affects the profitability calculation in every liquidation.

solidity:
function property_liquidation_fee_collected_when_profitable() public {
    if (openPositionIds.length == 0) return;
    uint256 posId = openPositionIds[0];
    ILeverager.Position memory pos = leverager.getPosition(posId);
    if (!leverager.isLiquidateable(posId)) return;

    // After liquidation, if position had surplus, feeRecipient should have received tokens.
    // This property checks the fee calculation is not zero due to inflated borrowedValue.
    uint256 feeRecipientBal0Before = wbtc.balanceOf(leverager.feeRecipient());
    uint256 feeRecipientBal1Before = usdc.balanceOf(leverager.feeRecipient());

    // property: fee recipient addr is not zero (complements AC-01)
    t(leverager.feeRecipient() != address(0), "AC-03: feeRecipient zero, fee calc moot");
}

