description:
Line 318 of `liquidatePosition`:

  uint256 borrowedValue = owedAmount * bPrice / ERC20(up.denomination).decimals();

`ERC20.decimals()` returns a `uint8` integer (e.g. 6 for USDC, 8 for WBTC), NOT the scaling factor `10 ** decimals` (i.e. 1e6 or 1e8).
Correct formula (consistent with every other location in the file):
  uint256 borrowedValue = owedAmount * bPrice / (10 ** ERC20(up.denomination).decimals());

With the bug, for USDC (decimals = 6), borrowedValue is over-counted by a factor of 1e6 / 6 ≈ 166,666×.  For WBTC (decimals = 8), the over-count is 1e8 / 8 = 12,500,000×.
`borrowedValue` is then compared against `totalValueUSD`:
  if (totalValueUSD > borrowedValue) { ...collect protocol fee... }

Because `borrowedValue` is inflated by ~12.5 million× the real owed amount in USD, this condition is virtually never true, so the protocol fee is never collected. Financially, this means the protocol silently foregoes all liquidation revenue.
Secondary risk: if `owedAmount * bPrice` is large enough that dividing by 6 or 8 causes an intermediate value to wrap (extremely large positions), the multiplication before the division may overflow in a Solidity 0.8 checked context, reverting the entire liquidation.

