In Leverager.liquidatePosition() (Leverager.sol:318):
  uint256 borrowedValue = owedAmount * bPrice / ERC20(up.denomination).decimals();
The denominator should be `10 ** ERC20(up.denomination).decimals()` (e.g., 1_000_000 for USDC) but instead uses the raw `decimals()` return value (e.g., 6 for USDC). For USDC (6 decimals): denominator is 6 instead of 1,000,000 — borrowedValue is 166,666x too large. For WBTC (8 decimals): denominator is 8 instead of 100,000,000 — borrowedValue is 12,500,000x too large. Compare this to _calculateTokenValues() which correctly uses `10 ** ERC20(token).decimals()`. Consequence: - `totalValueUSD > borrowedValue` is almost always FALSE (borrowedValue is astronomically large) - The `if (totalValueUSD > borrowedValue)` branch for protocol fee collection is never entered - Protocol NEVER collects liquidation fees, even when there is genuine profit - This deprives the protocol of all liquidation fee revenue Additionally, this inflated borrowedValue could be used as a guard for future logic.

