In Leverager.withdraw() (Leverager.sol:231-235):
  } else if (borrowed == up.token1) {
      uint256 repayFromWithdraw = amountOut1 < owedAmount ? amountOut0 : owedAmount;
The ternary uses `amountOut0` (token0 amount) when `borrowed == up.token1`. It should be `amountOut1 < owedAmount ? amountOut1 : owedAmount`. When token1 is the denomination and amountOut1 < owedAmount (not enough to cover debt), `repayFromWithdraw` is set to `amountOut0` (the wrong token's amount). Then `amountOut1 -= repayFromWithdraw` can underflow or produce wrong amounts. Additionally `owedAmount -= repayFromWithdraw` accounts a token0 amount against token1 debt. This means: - If amountOut0 > amountOut1: repayFromWithdraw is too large → amountOut1 underflows or
  the user repays less from their own funds than they should
- The final `ILendingPool(lendingPool).repay(borrowed, amountToRepay)` may be wrong
  since the pre-calculated amountToRepay was correct, but the actual denomination balance
  available is off.

