Strategy.checkPoolActivity() (Strategy.sol:310-342) iterates over pool observations. On line 322-324:
  (uint32 timestamp, ...) = IUniswapV3Pool(pool).observations(index);
  if (timestamp == 0) { revert("timestamp 0"); }
Uniswap V3 pools initialize with cardinality=1 but observations slots up to cardinality-1 may be uninitialized (timestamp=0) until enough swaps have occurred. When a pool has had its cardinality recently increased (e.g., increaseObservationCardinalityNext), new slots are zero-initialized. checkPoolActivity() will revert instead of returning false. Since openLeveragedPosition calls `require(IVault(lp.vault).checkPoolActivity(), "market too volatile")` and liquidatePosition does NOT call it (intentionally), a revert in checkPoolActivity() during position opening would permanently block new leveraged positions on that vault until all observation slots are filled by swaps. This is a denial-of-service on position opening, not directly an oracle price bug, but it stems from incorrect observation handling.

